# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so without this file the whole working tree goes
# into a PUBLIC image on ghcr.io. Two reasons that matters:
#
#  - Bulk. .git alone is 73 MB, tests 36 MB, docs and dev-docs another 4 MB. None
#    of it is reachable at runtime; the container imports emuses/ and runs
#    docker/startup.sh.
#  - Local builds. CI builds from a clean checkout, so it only ever sees tracked
#    files. A build run on a developer's machine sees the working tree, and this
#    repo accumulates run outputs there -- test_output/ was 9.1 MB of untracked
#    pipeline artifacts when this file was written. Those would be baked into a
#    published image. Excluding them here means the local build and the CI build
#    produce the same thing, which is the point.
#
# Keep in mind when editing: setup.py's find_packages() runs against whatever
# survives this file, and `pip install --no-deps -e .` happens at build time.

.git
.github
.gitignore
.codebase-memory

# Test suites, fixtures and their outputs
tests/
test_output/
test_data/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Documentation and project notes
docs/
dev-docs/
notes/
mkdocs.yml
CLAUDE.md
STATUS.md
CHANGELOG.md

# Superseded code, kept in the repo for reference only
legacy_archive/

# Build and packaging artifacts -- stale copies of the package shadow the real one
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
# ** is required: a bare `__pycache__/` matches only the context root, because
# Docker matches patterns against the whole root-relative path.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]

# Developer tooling that never runs in the container
scripts/
vscode_wsl_settings.json
get-docker.sh

# Compose stacks orchestrate the image; they are not part of it
docker-compose*.yml

# Local environment files. docker/.env in particular must never be published.
.env
*.env
docker/.env
